---
// PENDING PAGE (not built). Lives outside src/pages on purpose so the site builds before the game is synced.
// To publish: add the catalog entry and skill, then `git mv` this file to src/pages/math-games/. See the wiring steps.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GamePlayer from '../../components/GamePlayer.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import skills from '../../data/skills.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const game = games.find(g => g.id === 'subtraction-grade-1')!;
const skill = skills[game.skill as keyof typeof skills];
const page = new URL(url('math-games/subtraction-for-grade-1/'), Astro.site).href;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'LearningResource', name: `${game.keywordTitle}: ${game.title}`,
    url: page, description: game.summary, isAccessibleForFree: true, inLanguage: 'en',
    learningResourceType: 'Educational game', educationalLevel: 'Grade 1', typicalAgeRange: '6-7',
    teaches: 'Subtraction within 20', timeRequired: 'PT3M', image: new URL(url(game.poster), Astro.site).href,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Math games', item: new URL(url('math-games/'), Astro.site).href },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: game.keywordTitle, item: page },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Subtraction for Grade 1: Free Math Game for Ages 6 to 7 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="A free subtraction game for Grade 1. Kids send frogs off a lily pad, take away within 20 and light up the night. Friendly hints, no ads, no login, 3 minutes a game."
  path="math-games/subtraction-for-grade-1/" nav="math" image={game.poster} jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Math games', path: 'math-games/' }}
    items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Math games', path: 'math-games/' }, { name: game.keywordTitle, path: 'math-games/subtraction-for-grade-1/' }]} />

  <h1>Subtraction for Grade 1</h1>
  <p class="lead" style="font-size:20px;max-width:44em">Play <strong>{game.title}</strong>: help pond friends send
    frogs off a lily pad, take away numbers up to 20 and fill the evening with fireflies. Ten orders, about 3 minutes, free.</p>

  <GamePlayer game={game} topDifficulty={skill.topDifficulty} />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>What is Pond Hop?</h2>
    <p>Pond Hop is a short subtraction game for first graders, set at a pond at dusk where Hazel the owl keeps the
      lantern. A duckling, turtle, dragonfly or fish swims by and says something like "3 frogs are going for a
      swim!" Your child sends three frogs hopping off the lily pad into the water, and each leap makes a ripple and
      a gentle note. Then comes the real question: how many are still on the pad? Your child taps one of three
      answers. After ten orders the game ends with stars, and every solved order lights one firefly in the night sky.</p>

    <h2>What kids learn</h2>
    <p>The game practices subtracting within 20, a core Grade 1 skill. Children do not just pick an answer. They take
      things away with their own hands and see what is left, which is exactly what subtraction means. It moves
      through five small steps:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Taking away within 5, like 4 - 1</li>
      <li>Taking away within 10, like 9 - 3</li>
      <li>Keep a number: "Keep 5 on the pad. How many hopped away?", which shows that subtraction can ask for the part that left</li>
      <li>A teen number minus a small number, like 14 - 3, with fireflies in a lantern made of ten-frames</li>
      <li>Taking away across ten, like 13 - 5, by breaking the ten and counting what is left</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>Children in Grade 1, usually ages 6 and 7. It also suits Kindergarten children who already add with
      confidence and Grade 2 children who want quick, calm practice. Every order is spoken aloud, so early readers can
      play alone. It works best on a tablet, a computer or a phone held sideways.</p>

    <h2>How to play</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Press Play, then tap the big button to start.</li>
      <li>Listen to the order, then drag frogs off the lily pad into the water. You can also tap a frog and then tap
        the water.</li>
      <li>When the right number have left, tap how many are still on the pad.</li>
      <li>Not sure? Hazel helps. She points at the order, counts along with you, and never gives the answer away.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>A sky full of fireflies</h2>
    <p>Each solved order lights a firefly, and by the end of the game the sky glows. The fireflies belong to that game
      only, so there is nothing to lose and no pressure to come back. Your child's stars are saved on your own device,
      and the game never asks for a name, an email or a login.</p>

    <h2>Why it helps</h2>
    <p>The game starts easy and steps up after three right answers in a row, then steps back after two misses, so
      your child practices at the right level. Mistakes are never a dead end: if too many frogs leave, the extra one
      simply hops back, a wrong choice fades, and your child makes the last tap. Orders your child missed come back
      in the next game for another go. And because the wrong choices are real mistakes, like being one away or adding
      instead of taking away, the <a href={url('grown-ups/')}>Grown-ups page</a> can tell you what to practice together.</p>
    <p class="note">More to play: try <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a>, the
      other half of the pair, or see all <a href={url('math-games/')}>free math games</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <AdSlot id="game-bottom" />
</Base>
